        "Nothing about scheduling runs on India's clock — it runs on yours. Because the tutor is based in India, a lesson for a student in Toronto lines up with the tutor's own evening or early morning, and rosters are built around that gap rather than around ours. The stretch between roughly 4pm and 9pm local, plus weekend mornings, covers most Canadian bookings, and whichever zone you're in — including Newfoundland's odd half-hour offset — gets stated in full on every invitation, reminder and reschedule so nobody has to do the math themselves.",
        "The opening lesson works more like a diagnosis than a lecture. Rather than starting from page one of a textbook, the tutor asks what your child is currently being marked on, has them talk through two or three problems out loud, and listens for where the logic actually breaks down rather than just where the final answer went wrong. From there, most bookings settle into 60- or 90-minute sessions — 90 for IA work and past-paper practice, 60 for ongoing maintenance — each with a stated agenda, a stretch where the student does the working, and a short task to carry into the next one.",
        "Everything happens over video against a joint on-screen page that both sides can write on — equations and graphs for the sciences, tracked comments on a shared document for essay-based subjects. Past papers and worked solutions get marked up live and land in a folder your family can keep coming back to, and a session can be recorded if that helps with revision or lets a parent see how something was actually explained. None of it needs specialist equipment: a laptop or tablet, a decent connection, headphones, and a stylus if the maths gets heavy.",
